      "writtenOn": "2025-02-10T15:19:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ah OK - that makes a bit more sense. However, using `bytes_to_skip` in such a size check is probbaly only useful/consistent if we then used that same value for the reading, something like:\n\n```cc\nif (chunk_data_size \u003c bytes_to_skip) {\n  return ERROR_MALFORMED;\n}\nfor (int i \u003d 0; i \u003c bytes_to_skip; i++) {\n  if (buffer[i] !\u003d 0) {\n    return ERROR_MALFORMED;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nBut while we\u0027re hard-coding the `0` to `3` indices, i think we might as well hard-code the `4` too:\n\n```cc\nif (chunk_data_size \u003c 4 \n    || buffer[0] !\u003d 0\n    || buffer[1] !\u003d 0\n    || buffer[2] !\u003d 0\n    || buffer[3] !\u003d 0) {\n  return ERROR_MALFORMED;\n}\nAMediaFormat_setBuffer(\n    mLastTrack-\u003emeta,\n    AMEDIAFORMAT_KEY_CSD_0,\n    buffer.get() + 4,\n    chunk_data_size - 4);\n```",
      "parentUuid": "e5c57d5d_483534a4",
